# Writing an NMesh transport

A transport is the layer that moves bytes between two nodes. NMesh is entirely
agnostic about the medium: TCP, Bluetooth, LoRa, WebSocket, a serial UART,
carrier pigeons — anything that can carry bytes works.

---

## The two interfaces

### BaseTransport — one connection

Represents a bidirectional link between two nodes. One instance = one channel.
The transport must:
- delimit packets (framing) if the underlying protocol is stream-oriented
- serialise/deserialise Packet objects

```
BaseTransport
├── connect(address)   — opens an outbound connection
├── send(packet)       — sends a packet
├── receive()          — receives a packet (blocking)
└── close()            — closes the link
```

`listen(address)` stays in BaseTransport for the single-connection case (tests,
point-to-point links). For nodes that accept several clients, use BaseServer.


### BaseServer — several inbound connections

Listens on an address and creates a new BaseTransport per accepted client.

```
BaseServer
├── listen(address)   — starts listening (non-blocking)
└── close()           — stops accepting
```

The callback to set before listen():

    server.on_new_connection = async def(transport: BaseTransport) -> None

A server also carries five optional capabilities, which the core *asks* for
rather than reaching into your object. Most media implement none of them and
inherit the defaults; a datagram medium that wants NAT traversal implements
some. All five are documented on `BaseServer` in `src/transports/contract.py`:

| capability | default | what it is for |
|---|---|---|
| `bound_endpoint() -> (host, port) \| None` | `None` | where this listener can be reached |
| `send_raw(data, remote) -> bool` | `False` (does nothing) | one datagram out of the listener's own socket, for signals with no link yet |
| `holds(remote) -> bool` | `False` | is a link to `remote` already live here? |
| `adopt(remote) -> transport \| None` | `None` | a link carried by this listener's socket |
| `owns(transport) -> bool` | `False` | did this listener create that link? |

Never override one to do something a stream cannot: return the default and the
traversal it would have enabled does not happen, which is the correct outcome.

